# CHAPTER 2 · OPERATORS + TEXT OBJECTS

This is the chapter that separates vim users from vim power users.
Vim is a language: OPERATOR + COUNT + OBJECT. `d2aw`{normal} reads
"delete two whole words". Once you think in this grammar you stop
performing edits and start *saying* them.

## 2.1 THE OPERATORS

`d`{normal} delete · `c`{normal} change (delete + insert) ·
`y`{normal} yank · `gu`{normal} lowercase · `gU`{normal} uppercase ·
`g~`{normal} toggle case · `>`{normal} / `<`{normal} indent ·
`=`{normal} re-indent · `gq`{normal} reflow text.

Double any operator to act on the whole line: `dd`{normal}
`yy`{normal} `cc`{normal} `>>`{normal} `==`{normal} `gUU`{normal}.

## 2.2 TEXT OBJECTS: i = inner, a = around

Text objects describe WHAT, independent of where in it the cursor is:

`iw`{normal}/`aw`{normal} word · `iW`{normal}/`aW`{normal} WORD ·
`is`{normal}/`as`{normal} sentence · `ip`{normal}/`ap`{normal}
paragraph · `i"`{normal} `i'`{normal} quoted string ·
`i(`{normal} `i[`{normal} `i{`{normal} `i<`{normal} brackets ·
`it`{normal}/`at`{normal} HTML/XML tag.

The cursor only has to be ANYWHERE inside the object. No motion to
the start first. That is the whole superpower.

EXERCISE 1: Cursor anywhere between the quotes below, `ci"`{normal},
then type the corrected text exactly: hello world

print("helo wrold")

EXERCISE 2: Delete the call's argument list INCLUDING the parens with
`da(`{normal} (cursor anywhere inside them):

result = compute(temp_value, debug_mode) + offset

EXERCISE 3: Change the tag's content with `cit`{normal} (cursor
anywhere inside the tag) and type exactly: Neovim Power User

<title>Old Page Title</title>

EXERCISE 4: Empty the table below with `di{`{normal} — note it eats
the inner spaces too, leaving just the braces touching:

config = { retries = 3, timeout = 30 }

EXERCISE 5: Uppercase the whole line below with `gUU`{normal}:

keep calm and edit text

## 2.3 JOIN AND MICRO-EDITS

`J`{normal} joins the next line up with one space. `gJ`{normal} joins
with no space. `r{char}`{normal} replaces one character without
insert mode. `~`{normal} toggles case of the character under the
cursor. `o`{normal}/`O`{normal} open a line below/above.

EXERCISE 6: Put the cursor on the first line below and press
`J`{normal} to pull the second line up:

local widths = {
8, 16, 32 }

## 2.4 THE DOT COMMAND

`.`{normal} repeats the last CHANGE — operator, object and typed text
included. The professional loop is:

    make one perfect change → move → press . → move → press .

`ciw`{normal}new_name`<Esc>`{normal}, then on every other occurrence
just `.`{normal}. Design your edits so `.`{normal} can repeat them:
prefer `ciw`{normal} over visual-select-then-change, prefer text
objects over cursor-position-dependent edits. In chapter 4 the
`gn`{normal} object turns `.`{normal} into a guided missile.

Practice the loop below — change the first occurrence with
`ciw`{normal}, reach the others with `*`{normal} and `n`{normal},
repeat with `.`{normal} (not checked, just drill):

old_name = old_name + step
emit(old_name)

## WORKOUT

All six exercises, no instructions, under 45 seconds. Then in real
code tomorrow: catch yourself once doing visual-select-delete where
`daw`{normal} / `di(`{normal} / `dap`{normal} would have done it.

Key reading: [text-objects](text-objects), [single-repeat](single-repeat)

